## 6. State machines

### 6.1 Probe run

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> SELECT
    SELECT --> RESOLVE: 3 sealed goals admitted
    SELECT --> INSUFFICIENT_SOURCE: fewer than 3 sealed goals
    RESOLVE --> EXTRACT: SourceSet built for all 3
    RESOLVE --> INSUFFICIENT_SOURCE: a review artifact is missing/unreadable
    EXTRACT --> EXTRACT: next (goal, type, field) cell
    EXTRACT --> SCORE: census exhausted (267 cells enumerated)
    SCORE --> AGGREGATE: every cell has content_score + anchor + note-where-required
    SCORE --> EXTRACT: a cell lacks a required note (defect; re-open)
    AGGREGATE --> ADJUDICATE: ratios + type_presence + veto_failures computed
    ADJUDICATE --> EMIT: verdict fixed
    EMIT --> [*]: report written
    INSUFFICIENT_SOURCE --> [*]: report written with verdict NO-GO, basis = insufficient_source
```

`INSUFFICIENT_SOURCE` terminates as **NO-GO**, never as a missing file. A8 asks whether
PAEOS's exhaust is extractable; an exhaust that cannot be located is a stronger negative
answer than one that is merely lossy. Every state has an entry and an exit.

### 6.2 Verdict adjudication (C8)

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> VETO
    VETO --> NO_GO: any veto field absent in >= 2 of 3 goals
    VETO --> RATIO: veto set clears
    RATIO --> NO_GO: loss_ratio > 0.30
    RATIO --> GO: loss_ratio <= 0.30
    GO --> [*]: verdict GO (+ anchor carry per §4.3 if applicable)
    NO_GO --> [*]: verdict NO-GO
```

**The veto set** — the minimum fields without which SAEOS-1 §8's S2 exit criterion
(*"returns a real precedent from a real prior goal, within budget, with `why`"*) is
unreachable via `tradeoff_precedents`:

| Veto field | Why it is load-bearing |
|---|---|
| `Decision.label`, `Decision.rationale`, `Decision.chosen` | Without these the central node (✓1) is an empty shell; `why` (§5.1 rule 2) is unconstructible. |
| `TradeOff.axis_a`, `TradeOff.axis_b`, `TradeOff.resolution` | `tradeoff_precedents(axes)` matches on axes and returns the resolution. No axes ⇒ no method. |
| `Option.disposition` | §4.3: rejected options are *"the corpus's scarcest, most valuable knowledge"*. If chosen and rejected are indistinguishable, the Option corpus is noise. |
| `EvidenceRef.hash` **or** `EvidenceRef.uri` (either satisfies) | §3.3.3: SAKG emits pointers, never evidence. With no resolvable pointer the non-authority guarantee has nothing to point at. |
| Decisional edge family present (§3.3) | A Decision unlinked to its Options/TradeOffs is unreachable by every §5.2 traversal. |

**Threshold `0.30`.** Above 30%, more than one field in three across a node is missing or
degraded. §5.1 rule 2 requires every projection result carry an explainable `why`; a node
whose differentiating fields are mostly gone yields results that are formally explainable
but substantively empty — inadmissible under §3.3.2's untrusted-advisory framing, because
a consumer cannot tell a thin precedent from a wrong one. The threshold is a **judgment**,
carried as Assumption **A3** with its own falsifier, and the report publishes enough
detail (`band_a_loss`, `loss_ratio_review_only`, `band_b_loss`, `anchor_loss_ratio`,
per-cell ledger) that a founder can re-adjudicate at a different threshold **without
re-running the probe**. That property is deliberate: the threshold, not the measurement,
is the soft part.

**Veto precedes ratio, and is not a ratio.** A high aggregate score assembled from
peripheral fields must not outvote a missing `TradeOff.resolution`. This is the same
structure as PAEOS's gates: an aggregate cannot buy off a missing invariant.

